duramen 0.1

section SU "The suite"
  text
    The suite is what judges an implementation: one case per example, each a request line and
    what the response to it must hold. `cases` lists it, and `judge` compares an answer with a
    case (REQ-JU-001); running it against an implementation is not part of this document
    (OPEN-SU-001).

req SU-001 "No suite from a record with errors"
  text
    `cases` MUST answer `errors`, the number of errors `check` reports for the same record,
    and no cases when that number is not 0. Warnings do not stop the suite.
  example cases {}
    input files."s.duramen"
      frobnicate
    expect result = {"errors": 3, "cases": []}
  example cases {}
    input files."s.duramen"
      duramen 0.1
      spec s 1
      req A "a"
        text
          Nothing to show.
    expect result = {"errors": 1, "cases": []}
  example cases {}
    input files."s.duramen"
      duramen 0.1
      spec s 1
      oracle node echo.mjs
      op f
        input x? json
      req A "a"
        example f {"y": 1}
    input files."echo.mjs" from "fixtures/echo.mjs"
    expect result.errors = 0
    expect result.cases.0.id = "A#1"

req SU-002 "One case per example"
  text
    For a record without errors, `cases` MUST list one case per example and table row of its
    requirements: requirement by requirement in record order (REQ-RC-002), and within one, in
    order of lines. A case is an object with these members:
    - `id`: `<requirement ID>#<n>`, counting the requirement's examples and table rows from 1;
    - `kind`: `"example"`;
    - `reqs`: `["REQ-<requirement ID>"]`;
    - `platform`: the requirement's platform (REQ-SY-009);
    - `line`: the request line (REQ-SU-003);
    - `solo`: `true`, only for a case sent alone (REQ-OR-002), and absent otherwise;
    - `checks`: what the response must hold (REQ-SU-004);
    - `full`: the oracle's whole answer (REQ-SU-005).
  example cases {}
    input files."s.duramen"
      duramen 0.1
      spec s 1
      oracle node echo.mjs
      op f
        input x? json
      req A "a"
        example f {"x": 1}
          expect result.x = 1
      req B "b"
        on posix
        table f
          | x | result.x |
          | 2 | 2        |
          | 3 | ?        |
    input files."echo.mjs" from "fixtures/echo.mjs"
    expect result = {"errors": 0, "cases": [{"id": "A#1", "kind": "example", "reqs": ["REQ-A"], "platform": "any", "line": "{\"id\":\"A#1\",\"op\":\"f\",\"input\":{\"x\": 1}}", "checks": [{"path": "result.x", "kind": "eq", "value": 1}], "full": {"members": ["id", "result"], "result": {"x": 1}, "tolerances": {}}}, {"id": "B#1", "kind": "example", "reqs": ["REQ-B"], "platform": "posix", "line": "{\"id\":\"B#1\",\"op\":\"f\",\"input\":{\"x\":2}}", "checks": [{"path": "result.x", "kind": "eq", "value": 2}], "full": {"members": ["id", "result"], "result": {"x": 2}, "tolerances": {}}}, {"id": "B#2", "kind": "example", "reqs": ["REQ-B"], "platform": "posix", "line": "{\"id\":\"B#2\",\"op\":\"f\",\"input\":{\"x\":3}}", "checks": [{"path": "result.x", "kind": "eq", "value": 3, "from": "oracle"}], "full": {"members": ["id", "result"], "result": {"x": 3}, "tolerances": {}}}]}
  example cases {}
    input files."b.duramen"
      duramen 0.1
      req B "b"
        on windows
        example f {}
    input files."a.duramen"
      duramen 0.1
      spec s 1
      oracle node echo.mjs
      op f
        input x? json
      req A "a"
        example f {}
        example f {}
    input files."echo.mjs" from "fixtures/echo.mjs"
    expect result.cases.0.id = "A#1"
    expect result.cases.1.id = "A#2"
    expect result.cases.2.id = "B#1"
    expect result.cases.2.platform = "windows"

req SU-003 "Request lines"
  decision D-010, D-014
  text
    A case's request line MUST be, for a raw example, its line exactly as written. Otherwise
    it is a JSON object written without spaces between members: `"id"` (the case's ID) and
    `"op"` first; then the request members, which are the operation's `request` members if it
    has a `request` clause and the spec's otherwise, with the example's own on top, in the
    order ECMAScript keeps the members of an object (names that are array indexes first, in
    increasing order, then the others in the order written, a member set again keeping its
    place), each value written as `JSON.stringify` writes it; then `"input"`, last. Members the
    example's `omit` names are left out, and an example written `example <op>` with no input
    lines has no `input` (with input lines, its input starts as an empty object).

    The input MUST be written exactly as the example wrote it: every character of its JSON
    text, spaces and the spelling of numbers included; for a table row, `{`, then
    `"<field>":<cell>` for each non-empty input cell in the order of the columns, separated by
    commas, then `}` (so a row with no input cell sends `{}`). An example with input lines (REQ-SY-011) instead sends the compact JSON
    of its input, as ECMAScript's `JSON.stringify` writes it: the members of the example's
    object in the order written, then the members its input lines add, in order, a member set
    again keeping its place.
  example cases {}
    input files."s.duramen"
      duramen 0.1
      spec s 1
        request {"clock": "c", "trace": true}
      oracle node echo.mjs
      op f
        input x? json
      op g
        input x? json
        request {"mode": 2}
      req A "a"
        example f {"x" : 2.50e0 }
        example f
        example f {"x": 1}
          request {"trace": false, "user": "u"}
          omit clock
        example g {"x": 1}
          omit id, input
        example f {"x": 1}
          omit op, id
        example raw '{"id": "A#6",  "op": "f"}'
        example f {"x": 1}
          request {"b": {"z": 1, "10": 2, "9": 3}, "2": "two", "a": 1}
          omit clock
          omit trace
        table f
          | result |
          | ?      |
    input files."echo.mjs" from "fixtures/echo.mjs"
    expect result.cases.0 = {"id": "A#1", "kind": "example", "reqs": ["REQ-A"], "platform": "any", "line": "{\"id\":\"A#1\",\"op\":\"f\",\"clock\":\"c\",\"trace\":true,\"input\":{\"x\" : 2.50e0 }}", "checks": [], "full": {"members": ["id", "result"], "result": {"x": 2.5}, "tolerances": {}}}
    expect result.cases.1.line = "{\"id\":\"A#2\",\"op\":\"f\",\"clock\":\"c\",\"trace\":true}"
    expect result.cases.2.line = "{\"id\":\"A#3\",\"op\":\"f\",\"trace\":false,\"user\":\"u\",\"input\":{\"x\": 1}}"
    expect result.cases.3 = {"id": "A#4", "kind": "example", "reqs": ["REQ-A"], "platform": "any", "line": "{\"op\":\"g\",\"mode\":2}", "solo": true, "checks": [], "full": {"members": ["id", "result"], "result": {}, "tolerances": {}}}
    expect result.cases.4.line = "{\"clock\":\"c\",\"trace\":true,\"input\":{\"x\": 1}}"
    expect result.cases.4.solo = true
    expect result.cases.5.line = "{\"id\": \"A#6\",  \"op\": \"f\"}"
    expect result.cases.5.solo = true
    expect result.cases.6.line = "{\"id\":\"A#7\",\"op\":\"f\",\"2\":\"two\",\"b\":{\"9\":3,\"10\":2,\"z\":1},\"a\":1,\"input\":{\"x\": 1}}"
    expect result.cases.7.line = "{\"id\":\"A#8\",\"op\":\"f\",\"clock\":\"c\",\"trace\":true,\"input\":{}}"
  example cases {}
    input files."s.duramen"
      duramen 0.1
      spec s 1
      oracle node echo.mjs
      op f
        input files? object, n? number
      req A "a"
        example f {"n" : 1.50, "files": {"z": "old"}}
          input files."a.duramen"
            duramen 0.1
            "quoted" é
          input files.z
            new
        table f
          | n      | files |
          | 1.50   |       |
          |        | {}    |
    input files."echo.mjs" from "fixtures/echo.mjs"
    expect result.cases.0.line = "{\"id\":\"A#1\",\"op\":\"f\",\"input\":{\"n\":1.5,\"files\":{\"z\":\"new\\n\",\"a.duramen\":\"duramen 0.1\\n\\\"quoted\\\" é\\n\"}}}"
    expect result.cases.1.line = "{\"id\":\"A#2\",\"op\":\"f\",\"input\":{\"n\":1.50}}"
    expect result.cases.2.line = "{\"id\":\"A#3\",\"op\":\"f\",\"input\":{\"files\":{}}}"
  example cases {}
    input files."s.duramen"
      duramen 0.1
      spec s 1
      oracle node echo.mjs
      op f
        input t? string
      req A "a"
        example f
          input t
            x
    input files."echo.mjs" from "fixtures/echo.mjs"
    expect result.cases.0.line = "{\"id\":\"A#1\",\"op\":\"f\",\"input\":{\"t\":\"x\\n\"}}"

req SU-004 "Checks"
  text
    A case's `checks` MUST list its example's expectations in order:
    `{"path": <path>, "kind": "eq", "value": <value>}` for `expect <path> = <value>`;
    `{"path": <path>, "kind": "approx", "value": <number>, "tol": <tolerance>}` for
    `expect <path> ≈ <number> ± <tolerance>` and for a cell in a column with a tolerance; and
    for `expect <path> = ?` and a `?` cell,
    `{"path": <path>, "kind": "eq", "value": <the oracle's value at the path>, "from": "oracle"}`.
  example cases {}
    input files."s.duramen"
      duramen 0.1
      spec s 1
      oracle node echo.mjs
      op f
        input x? json
      req A "a"
        example f {"x": {"y": [1, 2]}}
          expect result.x.y.0 = 1
          expect result.x.y.1 ~ 2.1 +- 0.25
          expect result.x = ?
          expect id = "A#1"
        table f
          | x | result.x ± 0.5 |
          | 2 | 2.25           |
    input files."echo.mjs" from "fixtures/echo.mjs"
    expect result.cases.0.checks = [{"path": "result.x.y.0", "kind": "eq", "value": 1}, {"path": "result.x.y.1", "kind": "approx", "value": 2.1, "tol": 0.25}, {"path": "result.x", "kind": "eq", "value": {"y": [1, 2]}, "from": "oracle"}, {"path": "id", "kind": "eq", "value": "A#1"}]
    expect result.cases.1.checks = [{"path": "result.x", "kind": "approx", "value": 2.25, "tol": 0.5}]

req SU-005 "The oracle's whole answer"
  decision D-018
  text
    A case's `full` MUST be the oracle's whole answer to its request, as an object with:
    `members`, the names of the response's members, sorted by UTF-16 code units; `error`, the
    response's error, when it has one; `result`, the response's result, when it has one;
    `audit`, the response's audit text, when the operation declares an audit and the response
    has one; and `tolerances`, an object that maps each path in the operation's `tolerance`
    clauses to its number (`{}` when there are none, or no such operation). A raw example names
    no operation, whatever its line holds: its `tolerances` are `{}`, and it has no `audit`.
  example cases {}
    input files."s.duramen"
      duramen 0.1
      spec s 1
      oracle node echo.mjs
      op f
        input answer? json
        audit
        tolerance result.t 0.5
        tolerance result.u 0
      op g
        input answer? json
      errors
        e when never
      req A "a"
        example f {"answer": {"result": {"t": 1}, "audit": "A"}}
        example f {"answer": {"error": "e", "extra": 1}}
          expect error = "e"
        example g {"answer": {"result": 2, "audit": "B"}}
        example h {"answer": {"error": "e"}}
          expect error = "e"
    input files."echo.mjs" from "fixtures/echo.mjs"
    expect result.cases.0.full = {"members": ["audit", "id", "result"], "result": {"t": 1}, "audit": "A", "tolerances": {"result.t": 0.5, "result.u": 0}}
    expect result.cases.1.full = {"members": ["error", "extra", "id"], "error": "e", "tolerances": {"result.t": 0.5, "result.u": 0}}
    expect result.cases.2.full = {"members": ["audit", "id", "result"], "result": 2, "tolerances": {}}
    expect result.cases.3.full = {"members": ["error", "id"], "error": "e", "tolerances": {}}
  example cases {}
    input files."s.duramen"
      duramen 0.1
      spec s 1
      oracle node echo.mjs
      op f
        input answer? json
        audit
        tolerance result.t 0.5
      req A "a"
        example raw '{"id":"r","op":"f","input":{"answer":{"result":{"t":1},"audit":"A"}}}'
    input files."echo.mjs" from "fixtures/echo.mjs"
    expect result.cases.0.full = {"members": ["audit", "id", "result"], "result": {"t": 1}, "tolerances": {}}

open SU-001 "Running the suite"
  text
    How a suite is run against an implementation: the protocol cases on the stream itself
    (exit status, bytes, one response per request in order), which response is a case's answer,
    and the other kinds of case the rest of the language adds. How an answer is compared with a
    case is REQ-JU-001 to REQ-JU-004.
